id: decisions/003-rules-that-can-fail
title: "Every stated rule names what fails when it breaks"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'enforcement-mechanism'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: enforcement-mechanism
axis_gloss: "whether a rule is carried by code that can fail, or by prose that states the rule and relies on compliance"
poles:
  a: executable-enforcement
  b: documentary-declaration
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: full
leverage: 39
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(26) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.5)"

question: >
  When the repo writes a rule down, must something be able to fail if the rule
  is broken, or is the written record itself the authority and compliance a
  matter of reading?

why_it_matters: >
  The repo already learned once that a trailing YAML comment is not an
  enforcement: the epistemics commit blamed exactly that mechanism for the drift
  it then had to lock with a test, and the same comment-enum pattern still sits
  on five templates. Once answered, a bot reading core/ knows which rules will
  stop it (a validator will reject its record) and which are asked of it (a
  human will review), instead of treating every sentence as equally binding or
  equally optional.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    18 judgments sit at executable-enforcement (3 authored) and 8 at
    documentary-declaration (2 authored). The repository asserts both directions
    on this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave wants the relationship, agent and arena shapes in
    core/RELATIONSHIP_FIRST_AGENT_ARENA.yawn to get schemas eventually or to
    remain prose by design.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Every rule in core/, interface/ and automation/ names a test or
      validator that fails when it is broken. A rule with no check is labelled
      proof.status: asserted-unverified in the record that states it and may not be
      cited as proof by another record. Enum values live in a schema or a test,
      never only in a trailing comment.
    cost: "Schemas or tests for the constitutional shapes in core/RELATIONSHIP_FIRST_AGENT_ARENA.yawn (relationship, agent, arena), the seven-term credibility_standard in yawn.yawn, and the two human-judgment conditions in records/inquiry-aperture-one-question-face.yawn, which cannot be machine-checked at all."
    risk: "Subjective rules get fake tests, or get deleted because they cannot have one."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend scripts/validate-proof-refs.mjs (in-flight, uncommitted) so a status: active record under core/ or interface/ without proof.covers: fails; add an assertion that every 'status: x # a | b | c' comment in templates/ matches the enum the status-vocabulary test enforces."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0004"
      - "J0042"
      - "J0130"
      - "J0160"
      - "J0246"
      - "J0255"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: A .yawn record is the authority for what it states. Tests and validators
      are optional conformance evidence; a rule is honoured by being read, and proof
      blocks are narrative.
    cost: "Contradicts the stated rationale of tests/epistemic-status-vocabulary.test.mjs ('Free-text statuses make records uncomputable') and the in-flight covers:/not_covered: convention on core/canonical-extension.yawn."
    risk: "The next drift is invisible again, exactly as the 38-value status field was until 2026-09-12."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "None can enforce this; the record would be removing the 'must' from the credibility_standard and letting proof.status stay free text."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0191"
      - "J0442"
      - "J0536"
      - "J0661"
      - "J0912"
      - "J1124"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: A rule a program could check (a vocabulary, a required field, an
      authority flag, a path, a hash binding, an ordering) must be enforced by a
      named test or validator. A rule that needs a human (was the question worth
      asking, is the move bounded, is this an instrument or a prohibition) is exempt
      but must be listed under proof.human_review_required, as
      records/inquiry-aperture-one-question-face.yawn already does. Every proof:
      block on an active record carries covers: and not_covered: so the boundary
      between the two is visible.
    cost: "covers:/not_covered: lines on roughly 30 core and interface records; enum comments on five templates either gain a test or are moved into a schema; the constitutional shapes are explicitly listed as not_covered with a reason."
    risk: "not_covered becomes a parking lot; nothing forces it to shrink."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend scripts/validate-proof-refs.mjs: an active record under core/ or interface/ must carry proof.covers:; any not_covered entry must carry status and note (the shape already used on core/canonical-extension.yawn); a per-record not_covered count is frozen with a ceiling that may only shrink, following the grandfather pattern of tests/status-vocabulary.test.mjs."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0004"
      - "J0130"
      - "J0246"
      - "J0255"
      - "J0536"
      - "J0661"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.75
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    This axis is authored on both sides, but the two authored declaration-side
    judgments (the smallest move is an instrument, not a rule; 'did the question
    matter' is reserved for human review) are about which rules cannot be
    machine-checked, not against checking as such. The executable side holds the
    proof-attached and outcome-attached judgments and the suite's own rationale
    (GROUND_TRUTH FACT 7), and the in-flight covers:/not_covered: addition to
    core/canonical-extension.yawn (FACT 10) is already option C in practice. The
    genuine open cost is J0191: whether the constitutional prose shapes ever get a
    schema; C lets them stay prose while forcing the record to say so.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "Every status: active record under core/ and interface/ carries proof.covers: and, where needed, not_covered: or human_review_required:, and validate-proof-refs rejects one that does not."
    - "The five templates with trailing-comment status enums either match the enforced lifecycle enum or point at the schema that defines their values, and a test asserts it."
    - "yawn.yawn credibility_standard states which of its seven terms are required keys and templates/full.yawn carries them."
    - "npm test passes with those checks present."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A record carries proof.status: contract-defined or proof-attached while its
    named validator matches none of the claims under covers:, and the suite stays
    green.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0004"
  - "J0042"
  - "J0130"
  - "J0160"
  - "J0246"
  - "J0255"
  - "J0191"
  - "J0442"
  - "J0536"
  - "J0661"
  - "J0912"
  - "J1124"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
